ht olecranon with effusion. She has a family history of osteoarthritis.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of osteoarthritis and the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and using physical therapy.\n- Medical Treatment: We ordered a right elbow x-ray to assess the extent of the arthritis. We will refer her for physical therapy based on the results of the x-ray. We will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling.\n\n3. Psoriasis.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her psoriasis is under control. She has been compliant with her treatment.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue clobetasol for her scalp.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up of chronic problems.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nCarolyn Womack is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems. The patient is doing okay. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. This is mostly in her elbows. She has been sitting at the computer, at the desk more, in the office so she does not know if this is being stagnant in this position all day has increased flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that. She is right-handed and her right elbow bothers her more than the other, but primarily she is typing all day versus writing so it- it flares up in both. She denies any other joint pain. She does feel some numbness in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation. She denies any numbness or tingling in her feet. She has had a history of gout. The last episode she had was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe. She reports that the medication I gave her really controlled it and she has not seen a flare up since. She also reports that her psoriasis is under control. S